{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "406913046",
    "title": "TSD-SFCC-268 – PDP Maximum Purchase Quantity – BM Config",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/406913046",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 1,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "> **How to use this template:** Fill in each section in order. A TSD is not approved for implementation until Section 8 (Decision Record) is signed off by the Technical Lead.\n\n---\n\nDocument Information\n--------------------\n\n| Field | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Document ID** | TSD-SFCC-268 |\n| **Author(s)** | [Name] — Squad A / Technical Architect |\n| **Reviewer(s)** | [Technical Lead Name] — Technical Lead |\n| **Created** | 12 Aug 2026 |\n| **Version** | v0.1 — Draft |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Related Jira** | SFCC-268, SFCC-196, SFCC-250, SFCC-703 |\n\n---\n\nVersion History\n---------------\n\n| Version | Date | Summary of Change | Author |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| v0.1 | 12 Aug 2026 | Initial draft | [Name] |\n\n---\n\n1. Problem Statement\n--------------------\n\n### 1.1 Context\n\nThe storefront (PWA Kit on MRT) requires a mechanism to limit the quantity of any given product variant a shopper can purchase in a single order. This limit must be manageable by merchandisers without requiring a code deployment. The global maximum acts as the baseline fallback; per-product and per-customer-group overrides are addressed in SFCC-703.\n\n### 1.2 Problem\n\nThere is currently no site-level configuration in Business Manager that defines a global maximum purchase quantity per variant. Without this, the enforcement logic planned for the PDP (SFCC-196) and Cart (SFCC-250) has no config source to read from, making those tickets unbuildable. The configured value must be available to the PWA Kit storefront via the SCAPI product response.\n\n### 1.3 Impact if Not Solved\n\nSFCC-196 and SFCC-250 are blocked — neither PDP nor Cart enforcement can be implemented without a config source. Merchandisers have no ability to control purchase limits without a code change, creating both a delivery risk and an operational gap.\n\n### 1.4 Success Criteria\n\n1. A `maxOrderQuantity` site preference exists in Business Manager, editable by merchandisers without a deployment.\n2. The default value is `3` and the field enforces a minimum of `1`.\n3. The resolved value (product-level if set, site preference as fallback) is returned on the SCAPI product response as `c_maxOrderQuantity` and is consumable by the PWA Kit.\n4. When no product-level override exists, the site preference value is returned. When a product-level override exists (SFCC-703), it takes precedence.\n\n---\n\n2. Constraints & Assumptions\n----------------------------\n\n### 2.1 Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Detail |\n| --- | --- |\n| Platform | SFCC B2C Commerce + PWA Kit on MRT. No alternative platform is in scope. |\n| Storefront architecture | Headless PWA Kit. No cartridge-rendered storefront. All product data is consumed via SCAPI (Shopper Products API). |\n| Merchandiser access | The configuration must be updatable in Business Manager without a code deployment. |\n| Storefront scope | Solution must work across all storefronts (AU, UK, US Dom, US Intl, EU, ME). The site preference is per-site, supporting independent configuration per storefront. |\n| Enforcement scope | This ticket covers configuration only. PDP enforcement is SFCC-196; Cart enforcement is SFCC-250; per-product/customer-group overrides are SFCC-703. |\n\n### 2.2 Assumptions\n\n| Assumption | Risk if Wrong |\n| --- | --- |\n| PWA Kit consumes product data via SCAPI Shopper Products API (`/shopper/products/v1/products/{id}`). | If OCAPI is used instead, the extension point changes from a SCAPI hook to an OCAPI `afterGET` hook — see Section 3 for both options. |\n| Custom attributes prefixed `c_` on the SCAPI product response are already enabled for the site via the endpoint configuration. | If not enabled, `c_maxOrderQuantity` will not appear in the response and the PWA will always fall back to the hardcoded default. |\n| SFCC-703 (per-product `maxOrderQuantity` custom attribute) will be implemented after this ticket. The resolution logic in this ticket must accommodate it when ready. | If 703 is built without aligning on the custom attribute name and resolution order, enforcement logic in SFCC-196 and SFCC-250 will need rework. |\n| The agreed default value is `3`. | If the business default changes, the site preference value in BM must be updated; the code default is a safety net only. |\n\n---\n\n3. Options Analysis\n-------------------\n\n### 3.1 Options Summary\n\n| Option | Pros | Cons | Viable? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Option A** — SCAPI Hook (`dw.ocapi.shop.product.afterGET` on SCAPI endpoint) | + Resolution logic is server-side, not duplicated in the PWA + Single response field (`c_maxOrderQuantity`) for consumers to read + SFCC-703 override handled at hook layer with no PWA changes required | - Requires SCAPI custom hook registration and cartridge configuration - Hook fires on every product GET; negligible but non-zero overhead | Yes — Recommended |\n| **Option B** — OCAPI Hook (`dw.ocapi.shop.product.afterGET` on OCAPI endpoint) | + Same resolution logic as Option A + Familiar pattern if OCAPI is already in use | - Only valid if PWA Kit is consuming OCAPI Shop API, not SCAPI — PWA Kit default is SCAPI - Mixing OCAPI and SCAPI introduces dual maintenance overhead | Yes — if OCAPI confirmed |\n| **Option C** — Separate MRT API route fetching site preference independently | + Keeps product response clean | - Additional network round trip on every product page load - Config fetch must be coordinated with product fetch, adding complexity to PWA state management - Not necessary when hook approach cleanly solves the problem | No |\n\n### 3.2 Option Detail\n\n#### Option A — SCAPI Hook (Recommended)\n\nA SCAPI product hook intercepts the `GET /shopper/products/v1/products/{id}` response server-side. The hook reads the product's custom attribute `maxOrderQuantity` (set per SFCC-703) and falls back to the `maxOrderQuantity` site preference (set per this ticket). The resolved value is written to `c_maxOrderQuantity` on the response object. The PWA reads a single consistent field with no resolution logic client-side.\n\n#### Option B — OCAPI Hook\n\nFunctionally identical to Option A but targets the OCAPI Shop API product endpoint instead of SCAPI. Only applicable if the PWA Kit implementation has been configured to use OCAPI for product data rather than SCAPI. The hook pattern and resolution logic are the same; only the registration target differs.\n\n#### Option C — Separate MRT API Route (Discarded)\n\nDiscarded — introduces an additional network dependency on every product page load and requires the PWA to coordinate two async responses, adding complexity that is entirely avoidable with the hook approach.\n\n---\n\n4. Recommended Option & Rationale\n---------------------------------\n\n### 4.1 Recommended Option\n\n**Option A — SCAPI Hook**\n\n### 4.2 Rationale\n\nThe SCAPI hook places all resolution logic server-side, meaning the PWA Kit only ever reads a single field (`c_maxOrderQuantity`) with no branching logic. This keeps SFCC-196 and SFCC-250 simple to implement and ensures SFCC-703's per-product override is handled transparently at the hook layer without any future PWA changes. It is consistent with how other custom product attributes are extended in a headless SFCC setup.\n\n### 4.3 Trade-offs Accepted\n\nThe hook fires on every product GET request. The overhead is negligible (one site preference read + one attribute check), but it is a non-zero addition to every product response. This is accepted given the simplification it provides to all downstream consumers.\n\n---\n\n5. Technical Implementation\n---------------------------\n\n### 5.1 High-Level Sequence\n\n1. Merchandiser sets `maxOrderQuantity` in Business Manager under **Merchant Tools → Site Preferences → Custom Preferences**.\n2. Shopper navigates to a PDP or cart; PWA Kit calls SCAPI `GET /shopper/products/v1/products/{id}`.\n3. SCAPI hook fires on the product response.\n4. Hook checks `product.custom.maxOrderQuantity`: if set and greater than 0, uses the product-level value (SFCC-703 override).\n5. If no product-level value, hook reads `Site.getCurrent().getPreferenceValue('maxOrderQuantity')`.\n6. If site preference is also unset or invalid, hook falls back to hardcoded default of `3`.\n7. Resolved value is written to `response.object.c_maxOrderQuantity`.\n8. PWA Kit receives the product response and reads `c_maxOrderQuantity` — no client-side resolution logic required.\n9. SFCC-196 (PDP) and SFCC-250 (Cart) consume the value from the product hook to enforce quantity limits.\n\n### 5.2 Component / API Changes\n\n| File / Endpoint | Change Required | Type |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| BM Site Preferences — Custom Preferences | Add `maxOrderQuantity` Integer preference with default value `3` and minimum value `1` | New |\n| `cartridge/hooks/product.js` | Implement `modifyGETResponse` to resolve and append `c_maxOrderQuantity` to the SCAPI product response | New |\n| `cartridge/hooks.json` | Register hook: `dw.ocapi.shop.product.afterGET` → `./hooks/product` | Modified |\n| `app/hooks/useProduct.js` (PWA Kit) | Extend to expose `maxOrderQuantity` from `c_maxOrderQuantity` on the product response, with a safety-net fallback of `3` | Modified |\n\n### 5.3 Data Structures / Interfaces\n\n**Site Preference Definition** (registered via BM System Object Extension on `SitePreferences`):\n\nwidexml<preference-definition attribute-id=\"maxOrderQuantity\" type=\"int\">\n<display-name xml:lang=\"x-default\">Maximum Order Quantity</display-name>\n<description xml:lang=\"x-default\">\nGlobal default maximum purchase quantity per variant per order.\nActs as fallback when no product-level override is configured (SFCC-703).\n</description>\n<default-value>3</default-value>\n<min-value>1</min-value>\n</preference-definition>\n\n**SCAPI Hook — Option A (SCAPI endpoint)**:\n\nwidejavascript'use strict';\nvar Site = require('dw/system/Site');\nvar FALLBACK\\_MAX\\_ORDER\\_QUANTITY = 3;\n/\\*\\*\n\\* SCAPI hook: dw.ocapi.shop.product.afterGET\n\\* Resolves maxOrderQuantity and appends it to the product response as c\\_maxOrderQuantity.\n\\* Resolution order:\n\\* 1. Product-level custom attribute (SFCC-703)\n\\* 2. Site preference (SFCC-268)\n\\* 3. Hardcoded fallback\n\\*/\nexports.modifyGETResponse = function (product, response) {\nvar skuMax = product.custom.maxOrderQuantity;\nif (skuMax && skuMax > 0) {\nresponse.object.c\\_maxOrderQuantity = skuMax;\nreturn;\n}\nvar siteMax = Site.getCurrent().getPreferenceValue('maxOrderQuantity');\nresponse.object.c\\_maxOrderQuantity = (siteMax && siteMax > 0)\n? siteMax\n: FALLBACK\\_MAX\\_ORDER\\_QUANTITY;\n};\n\n**OCAPI Hook — Option B (OCAPI endpoint, if applicable)**:\n\nwidejavascript'use strict';\nvar Site = require('dw/system/Site');\nvar FALLBACK\\_MAX\\_ORDER\\_QUANTITY = 3;\n/\\*\\*\n\\* OCAPI hook: dw.ocapi.shop.product.afterGET\n\\* Same resolution logic as Option A; targets the OCAPI Shop API endpoint.\n\\* Use only if PWA Kit is confirmed to consume OCAPI rather than SCAPI.\n\\*/\nexports.afterGET = function (product, productResult) {\nvar skuMax = product.custom.maxOrderQuantity;\nif (skuMax && skuMax > 0) {\nproductResult.object.c\\_maxOrderQuantity = skuMax;\nreturn;\n}\nvar siteMax = Site.getCurrent().getPreferenceValue('maxOrderQuantity');\nproductResult.object.c\\_maxOrderQuantity = (siteMax && siteMax > 0)\n? siteMax\n: FALLBACK\\_MAX\\_ORDER\\_QUANTITY;\n};\n\n**Hook Registration (`hooks.json`)**:\n\nwidejson{\n\"hooks\": [\n{\n\"name\": \"dw.ocapi.shop.product.afterGET\",\n\"script\": \"./hooks/product\"\n}\n]\n}\n\n**PWA Kit — `useProduct` hook extension**:\n\nwidejavascript// app/hooks/useProduct.js (extended)\n// c\\_maxOrderQuantity is resolved server-side by the SCAPI hook.\n// The fallback of 3 here is a safety net only — it should never be reached\n// if the hook is correctly registered.\nexport function useProduct(productId) {\nconst product = useProductQuery(productId); // existing hook\nreturn {\n...product,\nmaxOrderQuantity: product?.c\\_maxOrderQuantity ?? 3\n};\n}\n// Consumption in SFCC-196 (PDP) and SFCC-250 (Cart):\n// const { maxOrderQuantity } = useProduct(productId);\n\n**SCAPI Product Response Shape** (with hook applied):\n\nwidejson{\n\"id\": \"product-sku-123\",\n\"name\": \"Product Name\",\n\"c\\_maxOrderQuantity\": 3\n}\n\n### 5.4 Error Handling & Edge Cases\n\n| Scenario | Expected Behaviour | Type |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Site preference is not set in BM | Hook falls back to hardcoded default of `3`. No error thrown. | Edge case |\n| Site preference is set to `0` or a negative integer | BM field validation (min-value: 1) prevents this at input. Hook also guards with `siteMax > 0` check and falls back to `3`. | Edge case |\n| Product-level `maxOrderQuantity` custom attribute not yet defined (pre-SFCC-703) | Hook reads `product.custom.maxOrderQuantity` which returns `null`; falls through to site preference. No error. | Edge case |\n| Hook is not registered or cartridge not included in site path | `c_maxOrderQuantity` absent from response. PWA `useProduct` safety-net fallback of `3` applies. Monitoring should alert on missing attribute. | Error |\n| Multi-site: different storefronts require different default limits | Site preference is per-site. Each storefront configures its own value in BM independently. | Edge case |\n\n### 5.5 Configuration & Environment Notes\n\n* Site preference `maxOrderQuantity` must be created on the `SitePreferences` system object in every environment (DEV, SIT, UAT, PROD) via metadata import or BM UI.\n* Default value of `3` must be set on import or manually in BM after creation.\n* The custom attribute `c_maxOrderQuantity` must be added to the SCAPI endpoint configuration (Open Commerce API Settings in BM) to ensure it is included in the product response. Confirm under **Administration → Site Development → Open Commerce API Settings → Shop API**.\n* Hook cartridge must be placed in the site cartridge path before any existing product cartridges.\n\n---\n\n6. Testing & Validation\n-----------------------\n\n### 6.1 Test Coverage\n\n| Test Type | What is Being Tested | Pass Criteria |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | Hook resolution logic: site preference value returned when no product-level override is set | `c_maxOrderQuantity` equals site preference value |\n| Unit | Hook resolution logic: product-level override takes precedence over site preference | `c_maxOrderQuantity` equals product custom attribute value, not site preference |\n| Unit | Hook fallback: site preference unset | `c_maxOrderQuantity` equals `3` |\n| Unit | `useProduct` hook: `maxOrderQuantity` exposed correctly from `c_maxOrderQuantity` | Returns resolved value; returns `3` when field absent |\n| Integration | SCAPI product GET response includes `c_maxOrderQuantity` with correct resolved value | Field present in response; value matches site preference when no product override is set |\n| Integration | Merchandiser updates site preference in BM; SCAPI response reflects updated value without deployment | New value returned on next product GET without code change |\n| E2E / Playwright | @p0 — PDP product response contains `c_maxOrderQuantity` equal to configured site preference value | Network response body contains correct field and value |\n\n### 6.2 Regression Risk\n\nThe hook appends a new field to the product response and does not modify any existing fields — regression risk to existing product response consumers is low. However, if the hook cartridge is incorrectly positioned in the site cartridge path, it could interfere with other product hooks. Verify cartridge path ordering in all environments after deployment. Existing product page rendering should be smoke-tested post-deployment.\n\n---\n\n7. Risks & Mitigations\n----------------------\n\n| Risk | Likelihood | Mitigation | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| SCAPI endpoint config does not expose `c_` custom attributes — `c_maxOrderQuantity` silently absent from response | Med | Verify OCAPI/SCAPI settings in BM before dev starts. Add integration test asserting field presence in response. | Tech Lead |\n| Hook name differs between SCAPI versions — hook does not fire | Low | Confirm hook name (`dw.ocapi.shop.product.afterGET`) against current SFCC version documentation before registration. | Developer |\n| SFCC-703 custom attribute name (`maxOrderQuantity`) misaligned — hook reads wrong attribute | Med | Agree and document custom attribute ID between SFCC-268 and SFCC-703 before either ticket enters dev. Record decision in Section 8. | Tech Lead |\n| Metadata import for site preference not executed in all environments | Med | Include preference definition in the deployment runbook. Validate in each environment as part of the release checklist. | Developer / DevOps |\n| Dependency on SFCC-196 and SFCC-250 not scheduled in the same or immediately following sprint | Low | Config-only ticket — can be deployed independently without user-visible impact. SFCC-196 and SFCC-250 are unblocked once this is live. | Squad Lead |\n\n---\n\n8. Decision Record\n------------------\n\n| Date | Decision Maker | Decision | Outcome / Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 12 Aug 2026 | [Name, Technical Architect] | SCAPI hook chosen over separate MRT API route for site preference exposure | Avoids additional network round trip; resolution logic stays server-side; single field for PWA consumers |\n| 12 Aug 2026 | [Name, Technical Architect] | Default `maxOrderQuantity` value set to `3` | Agreed by business / product team |\n| 12 Aug 2026 | [Name, Technical Architect] | OCAPI hook implementation documented as Option B for teams not yet on SCAPI | Functional parity with Option A; included to cover both API layers |\n| [DD MMM] | [Name, Role] | TSD approved for implementation | Approved — no changes required |\n\n### Sign-off\n\n| Role | Name | Date | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Author | [Name] | 12 Aug 2026 | Submitted |\n| Technical Lead / Reviewer | [Name] | [DD MMM YYYY] | Pending |",
